In the context of the Limosilactobacillus reuteri (formerly Lactobacillus reuteri) case study, the synthesis of reuterin and vitamin B12 highlights the highly strain-specific metabolic capabilities and health-promoting potential of different bacterial lineages. L. reuteri is a lactic acid bacterium adapted to the gastrointestinal tract (GIT) of vertebrates and is widely studied for its probiotic properties. To systematically explore this metabolic biodiversity, researchers utilized the Gempipe computational tool to reconstruct and analyze genome-scale metabolic models (GSMMs) for 545 filtered L. reuteri strains, evaluating traits across host-specific subspecies such as reuteri (humans and herbivores), kinnaridis (humans and poultry), and murium or rodentium (rodents).
Vitamin B12 (Cobalamin) Synthesis Vitamin B12 is an essential vitamin for humans that is typically acquired through the diet. Prior to large-scale genomic modeling, only four L. reuteri strains had been definitively identified as B12 producers as of 2018. Interestingly, during the Gempipe analysis, the 24 metabolic reactions that comprise the vitamin B12 biosynthetic pathway were intentionally removed before generating a "phylometabolic tree" used to cluster the strains. This exclusion was made because the capacity for vitamin B12 production is considered unrelated to the specific ecological niche or formal subspecies classification of the bacteria.
Reuterin Synthesis Reuterin is a mixture of different forms of 3-hydroxypropionaldehyde that is naturally released by several L. reuteri strains. It serves a critical health-related function by exerting antimicrobial activity specifically against Gram-negative bacteria. This targeted antimicrobial property makes L. reuteri particularly effective at combating gastrointestinal tract infections.
Shared Genetic Pathways and Strain Predictions Through the systematic evaluation enabled by Gempipe, researchers modeled the potential for synthesizing both reuterin and vitamin B12 across all 545 filtered strains. The simulations revealed that the majority—though not all—of the strains associated with humans and poultry possess the metabolic potential to produce both reuterin and vitamin B12. Furthermore, the synthesis of these two distinct health-related metabolites is genetically linked; the underlying genes responsible for their production are both located within the same pdu-cbi-cob-hem gene cluster.
The Larger Context of the Case Study The ability to produce reuterin and vitamin B12 is part of a broader investigation into how L. reuteri adapts to specific vertebrate hosts and exerts its probiotic effects. The case study also evaluated other strain-specific traits, finding that histamine production (which acts as an intestinal immunomodulator and anti-inflammatory agent) is an exclusive feature of the reuteri subspecies, with only a few strains lacking the trait. Conversely, urease activity—which converts urea to ammonium and CO2 to provide acid resistance—was predicted to be a survival mechanism predominantly utilized by rodent-associated strains. Ultimately, this GSMM-based computational approach provided a much more comprehensive, systematic evaluation of these health-related metabolites across hundreds of strains than could be achieved through traditional phenotypic characterization.
